  var D = {"states":{"new-jersey":{"name":"New Jersey","cap":36000,"capStatus":"rarely_applicable","ageLimit":"Coverage of behavioral interventions based on applied behavior analysis is required for an individual who is under 21 years of age and diagnosed with autism. The separate requirements to cover screening and diagnosis, and medically necessary physical, occupational and speech therapy, are not stated with an age limit in Bulletin 10-02's summary of the law.","visitLimits":"No visit or hour cap is set by Chapter 115. Carriers may not reduce the physical, occupational, speech or behavioral therapy benefits otherwise available under the contract because those services were used to treat autism or another developmental disability. A separate set of benefits may be established for these services only on terms the same as or more favorable than those applying to other conditions, and DOBI \"will not approve limits for physical therapy, occupational therapy or speech therapy for treatment of autism or other developmental disabilities that establish a maximum number of days of therapy measured from the date of diagnosis or inception of treatment, or benefit limits established on a per illness or injury basis.\"","selfFunded":"Yes, and it is the single most important thing to check before relying on anything else on this page. New Jersey's utilization management appeal rules and the Independent Health Care Appeals Program do not reach self-funded plans: DOBI states its answers \"do not apply to self-funded plans, to policies issued and delivered in a state other than New Jersey, or to limited benefits plans that do not provide hospital or medical expense benefits.\" The same boundary is why a self-funded employer plan is not obliged by Chapter 115.","medicaidAge":"Under 21. The state plan says these \"EPSDT services are available to Medicaid beneficiaries under the age of twenty-one with an ASD diagnosis made by a qualified licensed practitioner.\"","medicaidPA":"Yes, and where the request goes depends on how the child is covered. For NJ FamilyCare fee for service, prior authorization runs through the Interim Managing Entity on 1.844.276.2444, with a dedicated \"DMAHS and Autism Line (ABA & DIR Services)\" on 609.588.8522 or MAHS.ASDinquiries@dhs.nj.gov, and provider enrollment through Gainwell Technologies Provider Services on 1.800.776.6334. For a child in a managed care plan, authorization goes to that plan; UnitedHealthcare Community Plan, for example, routes ABA and DIR requests through Optum's Provider Express autism portal. Behavioral health access for children also runs through PerformCare on 1.877.652.7624.","medicaidRate":null,"flags":["not sourced. The Division of Medical Assistance and Health Services is the authority for NJ FamilyCare, and its official site publishes provider resources and service manuals but no adaptive behavior fee schedule. We could not reach a primary source listing a New Jersey dollar amount for the adaptive behavior codes, so we are not going to print one.","not sourced. Commercial insurers negotiate rates with each provider and do not publish them, so there is no primary source for what a New Jersey plan pays. Use the rate on your own provider quote or explanation of benefits rather than any figure quoted online, including ours."]},"georgia":{"name":"Georgia","cap":35000,"capStatus":"applies_to_aba_only","ageLimit":"20 years of age or under. SB 118 struck \"six\" and inserted \"20\": coverage is required \"for an individual covered under a policy or contract who is 20 years of age or under.\"","visitLimits":"None permitted. \"The policy or contract shall not include any limits on the number of visits.\"","selfFunded":"Yes. Georgia's mandate is written as a requirement on \"accident and sickness contracts, policies, or benefit plans,\" that is, on insurance. An employer that self-funds buys no such contract, so Ava's Law does not bind it, and neither does Georgia's independent review process, which applies to managed care entities regulated by the state.","medicaidAge":"Under 21. \"Autism Spectrum Services are for individuals under the age of 21.\" The manual adds that \"therapy can range from 10-30 hours per week, or more if medically necessary, and requires active parent/caregiver participation and involvement.\"","medicaidPA":"Required, without exception, and time limited. \"Prior Authorization (PA) is required for all Medicaid-covered ABS. Services without a PA will not be covered. ABS is authorized in six (6) month increments. All ABS PAs must be requested by the enrolled QHCP.\" A separate PA is needed for the assessment and for treatment.","medicaidRate":{"per_unit":18.69,"per_hour":74.76,"code":"97153","note":"Code 97153, adaptive behavior treatment by protocol, delivered by a U5 practitioner in the home, pays $18.69 per 15 minute unit."},"flags":["not sourced. Commercial insurers negotiate rates with each provider and do not publish them, so there is no primary source for what a Georgia plan pays. Use the rate on your own provider quote or explanation of benefits rather than any figure quoted online, including ours."]},"north-carolina":{"name":"North Carolina","cap":40000,"capStatus":"stale_base_indexed","ageLimit":"Coverage for adaptive behavior treatment \"may be limited to individuals 18 years of age or younger.\" Note the shape of that sentence: the age limit is something the plan is permitted to apply, not a statement that older people are excluded from the rest of the section. The screening, diagnosis and treatment obligation in subsection (b) is not written with an age limit.","visitLimits":"The statute sets no visit or hour limit for adaptive behavior treatment. Its stated limit is the annual dollar maximum in subsection (c).","selfFunded":"Yes, and North Carolina says so in plain words rather than leaving it to be inferred. The Department of Insurance states that external review \"does not apply to self-funded employer health plans,\" and the mandate itself binds \"insurance contracts,\" which a self-funded plan does not buy.","medicaidAge":"Under 21, through the EPSDT provision at subsection 2.2.1, \"EPSDT Special Provision: Exception to Policy Limitations for a Medicaid Beneficiary under 21 Years of Age.\" NC Medicaid also covers RB-BHT for adults: CMS approved the service for beneficiaries over the age of 21 effective July 1, 2021, \"when the intervention provided is supported by credible scientific or clinical evidence, as appropriate for the beneficiary's age range,\" and Clinical Coverage Policy 8F was updated to reflect it. North Carolina is unusual in this: most state Medicaid autism benefits stop at 21.","medicaidPA":"Required, and the authorization window narrows as hours rise. \"RB-BHT services are provided under a prior authorized Treatment Plan that has measurable goals over a specific timeline for the specific beneficiary being treated as developed by a Licensed Qualified Autism Service Provider (LQASP). Services provided without prior authorization are not considered for payment or reimbursement except in the case of retroactive Medicaid eligibility.\" For plans of 16 hours or fewer per week, Medicaid \"covers up to 180-calendar days for the initial authorization period\"; for plans of more than 16 hours per week, \"up to 90 calendar days.\" Reauthorization follows the same split, at least every 180 or every 90 calendar days. Being under 21 does not remove the requirement: \"the fact that the beneficiary is under 21 years of age does NOT eliminate the requirement for prior approval.\"","medicaidRate":{"per_unit":18.09,"per_hour":72.36,"code":"97153","note":"Code 97153, adaptive behavior treatment by protocol, is listed at $18.09 per 15 minute unit on the archived sheet, which is a non facility fee."},"flags":["do not publish a bare $40,000 figure. The statute permits a maximum benefit of \"up to forty thousand dollars ($40,000) per year\" for adaptive behavior treatment, but that number is a 2015 base that the statute then indexes: \"beginning in 2017 and for subsequent years, the amount shall be indexed using the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers for the South Region\" against the index as of March 2015, \"rounded to the nearest whole thousand dollars.\" Ten years of indexing means the figure in force today is meaningfully higher than $40,000. The current indexed amount was not published on any primary source located during this pass, so the guide must describe the mechanism and tell families to ask their insurer for the current figure, rather than repeating $40,000 as if it were live.","not sourced. Commercial insurers negotiate rates with each provider and do not publish them, so there is no primary source for what a North Carolina plan pays. Use the rate on your own provider quote or explanation of benefits rather than any figure quoted online, including ours."]}},"capCopy":{"rarely_applicable":"The cap is on the books, but your plan often cannot lawfully apply it. Ask the insurer to confirm in writing whether it is applying a dollar maximum to ABA, and why.","stale_base_indexed":"The figure in the statute is a base, not the live ceiling. It has been indexed for years and we could not find the current amount published anywhere we could cite. Ask your insurer for the current indexed maximum in writing.","applies_to_aba_only":"The cap applies to applied behavior analysis specifically, not to everything the mandate covers. A plan may apply it, so read your plan documents and raise federal parity if it does."}};
